'20260628175203982105_3a7dd11a', '20260628175204123729_30e0b298'], 'blocks': 
[{'text': 'The Ring of Nestor is a large gold signet ring associated with a 
sepulchral treasure of gold signet-rings and bead-seals reportedly found in a 
tomb at Thisbê (Thísvi) in Boeotia, Greece, and traditionally linked to the Late
Bronze Age cultures of the Aegean, especially the Minoan and Mycenaean 
worlds.[1][5][16][17] Evans and subsequent scholars treated it as a famous 
Mycenaean signet ring, catalogued as CMS I, 179 in the Corpus der Minoischen und
Mykenischen Siegel, and used it as a key piece of evidence for the study of 
Aegean religion and conceptions of the afterlife.[2][5][6] Modern discussions 
often place the ring within a chronological framework spanning Late Helladic I 
(early Mycenaean) and the Neopalatial period of Minoan Crete, but its 
authenticity and precise dating have been the subject of sustained scholarly 
debate, with some researchers considering it a genuine Bronze Age work and 
others arguing that it is a modern or “pseudo-Minoan” 
creation.[4][7][8][10][13][15] The ring’s elaborate miniature scene—featuring 
elements such as a female figure on a boat, altars, sacred trees, and standing 
sacred stones (baetyls)—has made it an influential and frequently cited object 
in studies of Aegean iconography.[4][5][19]', 'source_ids': 
['20260628175054384544_58f05803', '20260628175054419348_abfe9748', 
'20260628175152423614_76bffac6', '20260628175152471196_09198683', 
'20260628175152381686_6ef8d955', '20260628175152510223_2eedf316', 
'20260628175152553648_977827e4', '20260628175159168030_6bbf9c82', 
'20260628175159269958_24d76d9b', '20260628175159333010_cd136d36', 
'20260628175221524508_f38f67bb'], 'kind': 'paragraph'}]}, {'title': 'Discovery 
and provenance', 'section_type': 'section', 'disclosed_conflict_ids': 
['20260628175203982105_3a7dd11a'], 'blocks': [{'text': 'Arthur J. Evans’s 
monograph “The ring of Nestor. A glimpse into the Minoan after-world and a 
sepulchral treasure of gold signet-rings and bead-seals from Thisbê, Boeotia” 
presents the Ring of Nestor as part of a burial treasure of gold signet rings 
and bead seals discovered in a tomb at Thisbê (Thísvi) in Boeotia, central 
Greece.[1][5][17][18][20] In this study, Evans devoted the first section, 
entitled “Sepulchral treasure of gold signet-rings and bead-seals from Thisbê, 
Boeotia” (pages 1–42), to the archaeological context and detailed description of
the objects, and supported his account with five plates (I–V) illustrating the 
rings and bead seals, including the ring later identified as the Ring of 
Nestor.[1][17][18] Library and digital-library metadata, such as those provided 
by the Heidelberg University Library and the Aegeus Society for Aegean 
Prehistory, consistently associate the monograph with keywords including Nestor,
Ring, Minoan culture, Thísvi, and Kakóvatos, indicating that Evans’s account has
become a standard reference for the ring’s reported discovery and cultural 
setting.[1][17][20]', 'source_ids': ['20260628175054384544_58f05803', 
'20260628175152423614_76bffac6', '20260628175221412008_d63425e4', 
'20260628175221450188_ed9be685', '20260628175603970924_4fda2a33'], 'kind': 
'paragraph'}, {'text': 'Later discussions of the object’s provenance have raised
questions about the reliability of the traditional narrative. One synthesis of 
Mycenaean civilisation notes that the famous ring that Evans acquired, often 
referred to as the Ring of Nestor, is said to have come from a tholos tomb at 
Kakovatos in western Greece, but emphasizes that this attribution rests on 
statements made by intermediaries in the antiquities trade and is not supported 
by excavation records.[10][13][15] Another study on authenticity evaluations in 
Aegean glyptic art highlights the Ring of Nestor among “gemmae dubitandae” and 
stresses that, once the archaeological properties of such objects are subjected 
to close scrutiny, doubts about their authenticity and reported findspots tend 
to resurface.[10] Modern technological and stylistic comparisons with securely 
excavated gold rings from tholos tombs in the Volos and Dimini area further 
illustrate that the Ring of Nestor lacks the firm archaeological context that 
underpins the dating and cultural attribution of those other pieces.[13]', 
'source_ids': ['20260628175159168030_6bbf9c82', '20260628175159269958_24d76d9b',
'20260628175159333010_cd136d36'], 'kind': 'paragraph'}, {'text': 'A separate 
article on a Mycenaean bronze signet ring in the City Art Museum of St. Louis 
records that the ring it describes was presented to the museum by the dealer 
Kurt W. Bachstitz in 1924, documenting a transfer to that collection; however, 
the available summary does not identify this bronze ring with the gold Ring of 
Nestor, and the publication does not provide direct evidence linking the latter 
to the St. Louis museum.[19] Other bibliographic entries refer to the Ring of 
Nestor in connection with an Ashmolean Museum display caption dating it to about
1500 BC, but the summarized material provides only the caption text and does not
detail the path by which the ring entered that collection.[7][9]', 'source_ids':
['20260628175221524508_f38f67bb', '20260628175152589997_db5ebb4e', 
'20260628175152510223_2eedf316'], 'kind': 'paragraph'}]}, {'title': 'Physical 
description', 'section_type': 'section', 'disclosed_conflict_ids': [], 'blocks':
[{'text': 'Evans and later authors consistently describe the Ring of Nestor as a
large gold signet ring with an exceptionally large oval bezel.[4][16][17][18] 
Within the group of rings and bead seals from the Thisbê treasure, Evans 
distinguished a “large signet ring with an exceptionally large oval bezel” from 
three bead seals of flattened-cylinder type and from another, smaller ring, 
underscoring the object’s unusual size and form.[16][17] A modern discussion in 
the context of authenticity debates likewise notes that the ring is a gold 
signet with a very large oval bezel and raises questions about whether the 
manner in which the gold was worked and the linear engraving of the design 
correspond to Bronze Age technical practices.[4]', 'source_ids': 
['20260628175152381686_6ef8d955', '20260628175221372282_f25eeb76', 
'20260628175221412008_d63425e4', '20260628175221450188_ed9be685'], 'kind': 
'paragraph'}, {'text': 'According to one detailed re-examination of the object’s
material and dimensions, the Ring of Nestor measures approximately 3.29 by 2.18 
centimetres, making it the fifth-largest gold signet ring known from the Aegean 
world.[7] The same study situates the ring within a broader typology of 
Mycenaean and Minoan gold signet rings, emphasizing its substantial hoop and 
broad bezel as characteristic of a particular class of Aegean signet 
jewellery.[7][8]', 'source_ids': ['20260628175152510223_2eedf316', 
'20260628175152553648_977827e4'], 'kind': 'paragraph'}, {'text': 'Evans 
classified the ring’s form as a “type IV” golden ring with a hoop shaped like a 
thick reed-hammered roll, a type that he associated with Mycenaean signet 
practice based on comparisons with other rings in the Corpus der Minoischen und 
Mykenischen Siegel.[2] Later typological analysis remarks that this class of 
large gold rings seems to have continued in use into the historical period but 
had ceased to be worn after the Geometric era, suggesting a long but ultimately 
finite tradition of such signet forms.[16]', 'source_ids': 
['20260628175054419348_abfe9748', '20260628175221372282_f25eeb76'], 'kind': 
'paragraph'}]}, {'title': 'Iconography', 'section_type': 'section', 
'disclosed_conflict_ids': ['20260628175204123729_30e0b298'], 'blocks': [{'text':
'The bezel of the Ring of Nestor carries a densely composed scene that modern 
commentators interpret as a miniature ritual or religious tableau. One 
iconographic study describes the design as combining a female figure on a boat 
with architectural and natural elements, including one or more altars, sacred 
trees, and baetyls (upright sacred stones), arranged in a continuous frieze 
across the surface of the bezel.[4][19] This combination of motifs has been 
compared with other Aegean signet rings that show complex ritual imagery, and 
the Ring of Nestor has been cited as an especially elaborate example in which 
human and divine figures, cult installations, and natural symbols are integrated
into a single composition.[4][5][17][19]', 'source_ids': 
['20260628175152381686_6ef8d955', '20260628175221524508_f38f67bb', 
'20260628175152423614_76bffac6', '20260628175221412008_d63425e4'], 'kind': 
