{"accepted": true, "revision_requested": false, "severity": "clean", "report": 
"# Ring of Nestor\n\nThe Ring of Nestor is a large gold signet ring associated 
with a sepulchral treasure of gold signet-rings and bead-seals reportedly found 
in a tomb at Thisbê (Thísvi) in Boeotia, Greece, and traditionally linked to the
Late Bronze Age cultures of the Aegean, especially the Minoan and Mycenaean 
worlds. Evans and subsequent scholars treated it as a famous Mycenaean signet 
ring, catalogued as CMS I, 179 in the Corpus der Minoischen und Mykenischen 
Siegel, and used it as a key piece of evidence for the study of Aegean religion 
and conceptions of the afterlife. Modern discussions often place the ring within
a chronological framework spanning Late Helladic I (early Mycenaean) and the 
Neopalatial period of Minoan Crete, but its authenticity and precise dating have
been the subject of sustained scholarly debate, with some researchers 
considering it a genuine Bronze Age work and others arguing that it is a modern 
or “pseudo-Minoan” creation. The ring’s elaborate miniature scene—featuring 
elements such as a female figure on a boat, altars, sacred trees, and standing 
sacred stones (baetyls)—has made it an influential and frequently cited object 
in studies of Aegean iconography. [1][2][5][6][4][7][8][10][13][15][19]\n\n## 
Discovery and provenance\n\nArthur J. Evans’s monograph “The ring of Nestor. A 
glimpse into the Minoan after-world and a sepulchral treasure of gold 
signet-rings and bead-seals from Thisbê, Boeotia” presents the Ring of Nestor as
part of a burial treasure of gold signet rings and bead seals discovered in a 
tomb at Thisbê (Thísvi) in Boeotia, central Greece. In this study, Evans devoted
the first section, entitled “Sepulchral treasure of gold signet-rings and 
bead-seals from Thisbê, Boeotia” (pages 1–42), to the archaeological context and
detailed description of the objects, and supported his account with five plates 
(I–V) illustrating the rings and bead seals, including the ring later identified
as the Ring of Nestor. Library and digital-library metadata, such as those 
provided by the Heidelberg University Library and the Aegeus Society for Aegean 
Prehistory, consistently associate the monograph with keywords including Nestor,
Ring, Minoan culture, Thísvi, and Kakóvatos, indicating that Evans’s account has
become a standard reference for the ring’s reported discovery and cultural 
setting. [1][5][17][18][20]\n\nLater discussions of the object’s provenance have
raised questions about the reliability of the traditional narrative. One 
synthesis of Mycenaean civilisation notes that the famous ring that Evans 
acquired, often referred to as the Ring of Nestor, is said to have come from a 
tholos tomb at Kakovatos in western Greece, but emphasizes that this attribution
rests on statements made by intermediaries in the antiquities trade and is not 
supported by excavation records. Another study on authenticity evaluations in 
